  "worked_solution": "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
